== Team Bazaar ==

''Team Bazaar'' is a vibrant, decentralized crew of Tuxemon trainers, cyber-hackers, digital pirates, and liberty-loving wanderers who champion a world of open systems and shared knowledge. More than a faction, they're a philosophy in motion, committed to building a grassroots commons powered by creativity, mutual aid, and a sense of wild adventure.

=== Meeting Places ===

Their meeting spots are as unconventional as their thinking, forming ad hoc bases and pop-up labs in places such as:
* Hidden campsites with DIY antennas and solar rigs  
* Libraries and museums where they archive rare Tuxemon lore  
* University campuses pulsing with young trainer energy  
* Underground cafés full of encrypted signals and lively chatter  
* Pirate radio stations aboard drifting vessels, broadcasting Tuxemon sightings  
* Homes transformed into strategy dens and healing centers  
* Forgotten mansions reanimated into community hacker spaces  
* Markets buzzing with Tuxemon gear, battle rumors, and tales of the next encounter  

=== Philosophy and Activities ===

Within the game world, Team Bazaar members thrive through ''friendly duels'', not just for sport, but as rituals of respect and mutual growth. For fellow travelers contributing to '''Tuxepedia''', the digital encyclopedia of Tuxemon, they offer '''free healing'''.

=== Role in Spyder Campaign ===

In the '''Spyder campaign''', Team Bazaar is ''pretty loosely structured''. There are no rigid ranks or fixed roles, just a fluid swarm of passionate individuals, each carving their own path while remaining linked by trust, story, and tech. Players may encounter Team Bazaar members in out-of-the-way locations scattered across the region, places chosen not only for their quiet charm, but also for the tactical advantage of being off the grid and away from corporate eyes. These include:
* A semi-abandoned [[Mansion]] near Flower City
* Discreet cafés in towns and villages

These spots reflect Team Bazaar’s ideology: a deliberate retreat from monitored spaces controlled by entities like '''Omnichannel''', the dominant corporate power known for invasive tech and monopolistic data practices. Their preference for hidden corners isn’t just convenience, it’s resistance.